  "title": "Reading 1.1: Check the Number First",
  "text": "Control total: A figure you compute separately, such as a row count or a known total, and compare against what your analysis shows before you build anything on top of it. Why it matters: A workflow built on an unchecked extract produces a confident wrong answer, faster and at scale, with your name on it.\nA $218 billion mistake. New York City publishes the same fiscal year three times in one budget file: a preliminary version in January, an executive version in April, and the adopted version in June. Add them up without looking and the FY2025 expense budget comes to $327,314,366,109. The real adopted figure is $109,104,788,703. No warning appeared. No error was thrown. The chart looked fine. The data was genuine and official, and the answer was wrong by about $218 billion.\nFour rules that run all semester. Every number you present, you can trace: where it came from, what was filtered out, and what it counts. Check the number before you automate it. Speed makes bad data worse, not better. Unsigned is ungraded. If AI helped write it, a human reviews it, edits it and puts their name on it. “I don't know yet” is a professional answer. A confident wrong number is not.\nAdoption is broad; value is rare. In the survey the course uses, 88% of organizations use AI in at least one function and 79% use generative AI, but scaled agent use sits in the single digits. Only 39% report any enterprise-level profit impact. What is missing is rarely a smarter model. It is five unglamorous things: an agreed definition for every metric, a reconciled source of evidence, a verification step before anything ships, a named human at the approval gate, and a log that proves what happened. Those five are this course's syllabus."
