  "title": "Reading 1.2: Seven Kinds of System",
  "text": "Analytics vs. AI: Analytics turns data into insight so a person can decide what happened, why, and what is likely next. AI learns a mapping from inputs to outputs from examples, and can be wired to act on it. Why it matters: Classifying a system correctly tells you who is accountable when it is wrong, and what controls it needs.\nFour questions settle the argument. What does the system produce? Who chooses the next step? Can it act on its own? Who approves the action?\nSeven roles. \nAutonomy is a design choice. Think of four levels. Answer: no tools; a person does everything else. Assist: read-only tools; it can look things up. Execute: predetermined, low-risk actions inside explicit limits. Orchestrate: it chooses its own steps and pauses on anything high-stakes. Start with the least autonomy that delivers measurable value. A well-evidenced level 2 beats an unevidenced level 4."
