  "title": "Reading 2.1: Tokens, Context and Temperature",
  "text": "Token: A chunk of text, often part of a word. A large language model is trained to predict the next token, over and over, from hundreds of billions of examples. Why it matters: A model that predicts plausible next tokens is optimised for fluency, not for truth. Fluency is not evidence.\nPredicting the next word. Given “My favorite drink is”, a model learns that “bubble” and then “tea” are likely continuations because similar text appeared in its training data. Scale that up and you get systems that write convincing paragraphs about anything, including things they have no evidence for.\nContext is everything in the window. The model only knows what is in its context: your instructions, any documents you supply, and the conversation so far. Irrelevant details in that context can bias an answer, so a decision-grade prompt includes only the evidence that matters and says what to do when it isn't enough.\nTemperature controls variety, not truth. Higher temperature makes outputs more varied; lower makes them more repeatable. A low-temperature model that gives the same unsupported answer five times is not verified. It is consistently wrong."
