  "text": "API key: A long secret string that lets a program use an AI model on your account. Anyone holding it can spend your quota, and nothing asks them “are you sure?” Why it matters: Bots scan the internet for exposed keys. One screenshot is enough to leak one.\nSet it up the course way. Use a course Google account, not your USF account, and ideally not your personal one. Get the key from Google AI Studio. Don't click “set up billing” unless you have used up the free tier. If Gemini won't work for you, the guide's fallback is Groq. Do it at home, not on busy campus Wi-Fi.\nNever, ever. Paste the key into a chatbot to “check it”. Put it in a Canvas submission, a discussion post, an email or a screenshot. Post it on GitHub, even in a private repository. Share it with a classmate.\nWhat you submit. Only the last four characters. If you think a key leaked, delete it and make a new one; there is no penalty for that. And because free-tier prompts may be read to improve products, never send student records, customer names or an employer's data to any AI tool."
