  "text": "What-if analysis: Recalculating results under a decision you haven't made yet, here a maximum discount, to compare options before acting. Why it matters: It turns “we should discount less” into a specific cap with a specific effect on profit and sales.\nThree formulas. List Price = Sales ÷ (1 − Discount): the price before the discount. Adjusted Sales = List Price × (1 − min(Discount, Cap)): undo the discount, apply the capped one. Adjusted Profit = Profit + (Adjusted Sales − Sales): cost doesn't change, so each extra dollar of sales is an extra dollar of profit.\nThe Texas class example. Texas Binders sold at an 80% average discount: $9,043 in sales and a $14,705 loss. At a 45% cap, adjusted sales are $24,867 and profit turns to $1,120. Appliances at a 20% cap move from −$6,147 to $1,076."
